president ramaphosa thank you so much  for speaking to dw news after the aueu  summit here in brussels  talking about this trips waiver you have  come to brussels with the intention of  persuading the europeans to change their  vision  and to release the ip on the  intellectual property on vaccines so  that they can be produced in africa  germany is one of the countries that is  most reluctant to do so how much of your  anger towards the europeans is directed  towards germany and what does this mean  for africa that this trips waiver isn't  coming into place  well on the trip's waiver issue it's not  so much anger that is directed against  anyone or in particular germany  i had a wonderful meeting with the new  chancellor  mr scholz and we had a good exchange on  the issue of  the  ip waiver  and i explained to him what  is driving us  to  put forward the arguments that we are  putting forward  and i said in our view when we are in a  pandemic like this  what  will get us out of the pandemic should  be regarded as a public good it should  not be seen as a  profiteering type of  instrument or product it should be  what should be readily available  and i think that message got through to  him  and he started arguing that you know  even the originators don't necessarily  want to make money out of it i said well  then  we've got two  uh  objectives that that can be put to to  good use for us to reach agreement  and obviously they want the originators  want to make  to do more and more research  and i said there are ways in which we  can we can meet that objective by  reaching a consensus  on the ip as it stands so that  countries that are able to utilize that  ip  and south africa being one of them  should be able to utilize the ip to  manufacture more  and more vaccines and i did say that  we we don't want to be receiving crumbs  from anybody's table  we we don't just want to fill and finish  where we get the drug substance from  somewhere fill it and then put it on the  market we want to be able to manufacture  the drug substance ourselves and that is  where the real heart of the ib and the  ip  argument comes in  and i said  that should be fairly straightforward  and then again i argued  that  this is not for ever type of provision  we want it for a set period  and so that we're able to get over this  vaccine  so i think that message got through and  it is for that reason that  we've it's now been felt that  it should be there should be a two  process uh negotiation at the  commissions  and thereafter should then be transposed  to the wto itself  with the active participation of the  director general of the wto by the way  dr ngozi the director general is very  keen  that a solution should be found  that a compromise should be found  because this is the type of pandemic  that requires  both sides to get together and find  agreement  now what happened at the end of this  summit is that there's six hubs that are  going to be created in africa one of  them  in south africa it's sort of been  accepted but as a positive step  but as you mentioned this is sort of a  fundamental  issue between africa and europe this  idea of the relationship and this summit  was about trying to  change the relationship  does this issue to you highlight the  real fundamental problems in the  relationship between africa and europe  i would say the reluctance  of  europe for instance to agree  to reach an agreement on the trip's  waiver  it's what weakens the relationship  an agreement on the trip's waiver would  strengthen the relationship would  consolidate it  and i did say as much in the meeting  that  if we are not able to reach agreement on  this  it would be seen that this was a failure  however a proposal was put on the table  to  to try and find another avenue of of  addressing this matter and  i look forward to seeing whether a  solution will be found  because if we're not able to find a  solution on this despite everything else  that is being done in the way of  making vaccines available to africa  uh the the the hubs that are being set  up  it will mean that we have not really  been able to find each other on a matter  of life and death  a key matter  where we should be embracing one another  and finding one another the rest of the  other things can happen  but if they find people dead  what is the use  of all that  so we need then to do things in tandem  and all at the same time and be able to  demonstrate that our partnership the  value of our partnership  is important  do you approve of  france's withdrawal from mali or are you  worried that this is going to have a  major security effect in africa  well  this has been discussed it it obviously  the withdrawal is going to have a  negative effect  but at the same time  france is motivated by a whole range of  issues including i think their own  domestic issues  so one needs to be considerate of that  but  the security issue in the sahel  is a major major problem and  the the assistance and the support  of  other countries such as france has been  of great help  and  when we come to security matters  there's a need to cooperate thank you so  much president ronaldo
 

